# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# /widget.js is the embed entry point: third-party pages hardcode <script src=".../widget.js">, so it
# CAN'T be content-hashed like the app's chunks. Pages' default for static assets is
# `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate` (4h) — which pins embedders (and our own demo) to a stale
# widget for up to 4 hours after every deploy. `no-cache` keeps the browser copy but forces a revalidate
# on each load: a cheap 304 (via ETag) when unchanged, and the fresh bundle the moment a deploy changes
# it. min-version.json is the kill-switch manifest and must be fresh for the same reason.
/widget.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/min-version.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Declare UTF-8 explicitly on text assets. The app + widget bundles hold Hebrew + emoji (i18n tables, agent
# labels), and Cloudflare Pages serves them as `application/javascript` with NO charset — a browser/WebView
# that doesn't inherit the page's encoding would mojibake them. HTML already carries charset=utf-8.
/*.js
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8
/*.mjs
  Content-Type: application/javascript; charset=utf-8
/*.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
/*.css
  Content-Type: text/css; charset=utf-8

# Baseline security headers for every response. nosniff stops MIME-confusion XSS; Referrer-Policy keeps the
# full URL (a /j-hop link carries gate params on the query) out of cross-origin Referers. A Content-Security-
# Policy is a separate, larger task: it must enumerate the app's signal/TURN/OIDC/JWKS/widget origins, account
# for the embeddable widget + inline styles, and ship Report-Only first to confirm it doesn't break the app.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
